# Shell reaches the working tree with LF, always.
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true in its system config, so without this a Windows
# checkout holds CRLF hooks. That is harmless while they run under Git Bash — and fatal the
# moment the same checkout is opened from WSL or a Linux container: `dash` reads the trailing
# CR as part of the last token (`set: Illegal option -`) and exits 2. A PreToolUse hook that
# exits 2 does not merely fail, it BLOCKS the tool call — so every Bash command in the session
# dies, with an error that points at the shell rather than at line endings. Neither CI (LF
# runners) nor the smoke suite (MSYS `sh` tolerates the CR) can see it (attest ADR-0039).
*.sh            text eol=lf
.claude/hooks/* text eol=lf
